\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot 270=24\\,300.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120643/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120643"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-274769/field-1/0e4e79001619645600fe317a8a7dc3e7c54e40dedca3eec2190744766458f0b4" :answer/id #uuid "0712c4ec-45ce-5554-a56d-c288e20ff1a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274769/field-1/1dee3a8b4e2e69484264522b7c3f6207b09ecde7ad469649dd240ad6a8e6346d" :answer/id #uuid "de66e918-6cf1-5dcf-8eb9-eb8ae53948c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274769/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "65f24566-d3c7-5af3-ba11-2328b1ea39b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274769/field-1/0e4e79001619645600fe317a8a7dc3e7c54e40dedca3eec2190744766458f0b4" :answer-field/choices ["q-274769/field-1/0e4e79001619645600fe317a8a7dc3e7c54e40dedca3eec2190744766458f0b4" "q-274769/field-1/1dee3a8b4e2e69484264522b7c3f6207b09ecde7ad469649dd240ad6a8e6346d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274769/field-2/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77" :answer/id #uuid "5c6b468a-4f69-573b-9bcb-a7b9c9186bd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-274769/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "55f247c3-51b0-56e8-b8b0-611695eb29a6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-274769/field-2/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77" :answer-field/choices ["q-274769/field-2/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-274769" :question/id #uuid "8b6e07f5-0e84-5942-8281-b6d656744b79" :question/math-academy-id "q-274769" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3=3+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3 & =3+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-274769/field-1" "q-274769/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-274769"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-330707/field-1/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77" :answer/id #uuid "6a4d0b1a-2390-539c-b86a-bd8f1d3b3a06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330707/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f5458c8-33ea-50f8-b914-294bdb03eaa3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330707/field-1/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77" :answer-field/choices ["q-330707/field-1/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330707/field-2/82a873b6b3ece3b4059b2da1839886387bbd4d4e56d0ad136fc169204f9d3030" :answer/id #uuid "ec37de83-979e-562b-9b95-7288a19c6666" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330707/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "77865180-5e60-592d-96dd-f7706d106ef4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330707/field-2/82a873b6b3ece3b4059b2da1839886387bbd4d4e56d0ad136fc169204f9d3030" :answer-field/choices ["q-330707/field-2/82a873b6b3ece3b4059b2da1839886387bbd4d4e56d0ad136fc169204f9d3030"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330707" :question/id #uuid "52aca61e-02c9-5f63-9795-8391b2d85977" :question/math-academy-id "q-330707" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7$ for $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-330707/field-1" "q-330707/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-330707"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3537"] :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x <  360^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-30^\\circ$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution. However, we can generate solutions by adding integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =x+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{30}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={150}^{∘} \\\\  \\\\ {x}_{2} & =x+{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=150^\\circ, 330^\\circ.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1 \\cdot x_2$ is $150 \\cdot 330 = 49\\,500.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4266"] :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation  $2\\cot x =9 - \\cot x$ for $0 \\leq x <                         2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2,$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =9-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =9 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =\\dfrac{1}{3} .\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(\\frac{1}{3})=0.321\\,750≈0.321\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.321\\,8$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 0.321\\,8.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi +0.321\\,8 \\\\  & =3.463\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n0.321\\,8 \\cdot 3.463\\,4 \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3539"] :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $-180^\\circ <                      x <                      180^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-30^\\circ$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 =-30^\\circ .$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{30}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={150}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x= -30^\\circ, 150^\\circ .$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is \n\n$$\n-30\\cdot(150) = -4\\,500.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7699"] :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3- 2\\cot x =3$ for $0^\\circ <                           x <                           360^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{1}{0} = \\textrm{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\cot x = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\cos x &=0\\[5pt]\nx &= \\arccos 0 \\[5pt]\nx &= 90^\\circ\n}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 90^\\circ.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=90^\\circ, 270^\\circ.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is \n\n$$\n90 \\cdot(270) = 24\\,300.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
